A||N||9||ur2biteme||HELP!!  i think i blew a tranny seal!  i'm stranded!||||5||
Z||000000||ur2biteme||10-23-2000||07:03 AM||||so, i'm driving one of my normal deliveries, making a left hand turn.  let out the clutch in 1st, get to about 12 mph, then CLUNK, fairly loud, and grinding sound starts.  the grinding matches vehicle speed.  pulled over to the side of the road, transmission fluid POURING out.  everything is shifting fine, babying it back to the store anyway.  clutch working fine as well, no excess slipping, no extra effort (isuzu 5 speed).  park it there, work inhouse for 3 hours.  go to baby it home.  same thing, operates properly, grinding noise matching vehicle speed, still shifts into all gears, smoothly.  drive it no more than 15 mph home, where it sits now.  <P>here's the rundown.  <BR>*the inital slam, and loud clunk.<BR>*no more transmission fluid.<BR>*car jerked 2, maybe 3 times while driving afterward.<BR>*grinding noise matching vehicle speed<BR>*isuzu 5 speed, clutch working properly, shifting smoothly and cleanly even with no fluid.<BR>*noticed that the fluid leak probably started around the passenger side axle, since the inboard c.v. joint is covered in the tranny oil.<P>can i fix it without replacing the tranny?<BR>can i fix it without dropping the cradle?<P>help! i have no transportation!  <BR>guess i jinxed myself with my signature.  lol<BR>thanks in advance.<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||10-23-2000||09:13 AM||||Oops! Sounds like serious trouble inside the transmission.  Maybe a differential bearing has gone south.  If it is a bearing, then the axle probably has too much play and will wipe out a replacement seal.  I know that this isn't what you want to hear, but it sounds like transmission time to me.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE<p>[This message has been edited by Rodrv6 (edited 10-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||10-23-2000||10:27 AM||||I suppose, if you're lucky, it could be the CV joint just popped out of the side of the transaxle, enough to let fluid leak out, but not enough to disengage the splines?  Might try (carefully) popping it back in.<P>If that doesn't work, the solution will probably take awhile  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <BR> ||||reg||9
Z||000003||ur2biteme||10-23-2000||12:19 PM||||well, since this is isuzu #3 in the car, (i don't drive that hard!) what is the most bulletproof transmision?  i'm getting sick of these $6-700 a year repairs!  i want a tranny that's gonna last more than 30k miles!  (starting to get rather irritated with this thing!)||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||10-23-2000||01:29 PM||||Ouch!  Are you sure it's the tranny, or maybe the installer?  Was it the same installer each time? No idea, of course, but it makes me wonder-?<P>My guess is the Getrag 5-speeds/ Muncie 4-speeds used with the V6 are the most bulletproof manuals in the Fiero.  What engine do you have?  I'm interested, because this is one of the most anti-Isuzu testimonials I've read here.  Isuzu #3?  Good grief   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> !  <P>If you do a lot of stop-and-go (deliveries), might think about going with an automatic.  The easiest way is to get a different Fiero.     <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-23-2000).]||||reg||11
Z||000005||ur2biteme||10-23-2000||09:00 PM||||stu:  yes it is the same installer, but i've talked with him each time the stuff happens and he shows me everything that went wrong on every occasion.  very descriptive and knowlegable.  i havent had clutch problems since (the original problem) and he is extremely knowlegable about fieros.  i didn't know about the clutch pedal bending problem until this shop.  i'm pretty confident it's not the installer.  <P>i have an over-achieving duke!  that's what seems to keep killing the trannys.  i'd go auto, but i don't like them.  it just seems that going to a good manual should be just as reliable, or more reliable than an auto.<P>what would be involved in swapping to a 4 speed muncie?  i seem to recall they are almost as easy to get as the isuzu, and sound virtually bulletproof (heard about the synchro problems in the getrag).  i'm hoping it doesnt involve a different clutch, because this one is under warranty and any excessive wear will be replaced with new.  i'm thinking that i'll need the cables, shifter, tranny and possibly hydraulics from the donor.  help!!  lol<P>thanks for the help so far.   <P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||10-23-2000||10:11 PM||||Reminds me of that joke about the penguin eating a vanilla ice cream cone while the mechanic worked on his car...<P>"Looks like you blew a seal."<BR>"No, it's just ice cream."<P>I slay myself...||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||10-24-2000||12:58 AM||||If it were my car, I would go with the Muncie. I have a Getrag in my '88 Formula, and I have never been very pleased with the tranny. I have plans to install a Muncie in hte car sometime early next year(if I ever get around to rebuilding the 2.8 in it).<P>I have only driven a Muncie tranny a couple of time,  but that was the smoothest shifting tranny I have ever driven. I have a Muncie in my '86SE, but haven't adjusted the cables on it yet, so don't know how it shifts.||||reg||1
